== Chapter XIV

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[The general use of the foregoing directions — The great direction for accomplishing the work aimed at: Exercise faith on Christ — The several ways this may be done — Consideration of the fullness in Christ for relief proposed — Great expectations from Christ — Grounds of these expectations: his mercifulness, his faithfulness — What comes of such expectations: on the part of Christ; on the part of believers — Faith to be exercised especially on the death of Christ, Rom 6:3–6 — The work of the Spirit in this whole business.]

#smallcaps[Now], the considerations I have dwelt on so far are things #emph[preparatory] to the work aimed at, rather than things that will #emph[accomplish] it. What I have aimed at so far is the heart’s proper preparation for the work itself, without which it will not be accomplished.

The directions for the work itself are very few; I mean those that belong particularly to it. They are these:

==== The Work Itself: Exercise Faith on Christ

1\. Set faith to work on Christ for the #emph[killing] of your sin. His blood is the great sovereign remedy for sin-sick souls. Live in this, and you will die a conqueror; indeed, through the good providence of God, you will live to see your lust dead at your feet.

But you will say, “How shall faith act on Christ for this end and purpose?” I say, in several ways:

(1.) By faith fill your soul with a proper consideration of the #emph[provision] that is laid up in Jesus Christ for this end and purpose: that all your lusts, this very lust you are entangled with, may be mortified. By faith ponder this: though you are in no way able in or by yourself to get the victory over your disorder, though you are even weary of fighting and utterly ready to faint, yet there is enough in Jesus Christ to give you relief (Phil 4:13). It held up the prodigal, when he was#footnote[Luke 15:17.] ready to faint, that there was still bread enough in his father’s house. Though he was far from it, it relieved him and held him up that it was there. In your greatest distress and anguish, consider the fullness of grace, the riches, the#footnote[Isa 40:28–31.] treasures of strength, might, and help that are laid up in him for our support (John 1:16; Col 1:19). Let them come into your mind and stay there. Consider that he is “exalted … as Prince and Savior, in order to grant repentance … to Israel” (Acts 5:31); and if to grant repentance, then to grant mortification, without which repentance does not and cannot exist. Christ tells us that we obtain purging grace by abiding in him (John 15:3). To exercise faith on the fullness that is in Christ for our supply is an outstanding way of abiding in Christ; for both our being grafted in and our abiding are by faith (Rom 11:19, 20). So let your soul be exercised by faith with thoughts and convictions such as these: “I am a poor, weak creature. Unstable as water, I cannot excel. This corruption is too hard for me, and it is at the very point of ruining my soul; and I do not know what to do. My soul has become like parched ground, a haunt of jackals. I have made promises and broken them; vows and commitments have come to nothing. Many times I have been persuaded that I had won the victory and would be delivered, but I have been deceived. So I see plainly that without some great help and aid I am lost, and shall be brought at last to abandon God altogether. Yet, though this is my state and condition, let the hands that hang down be lifted up, and the feeble knees be strengthened. Look:#footnote[John 1:16; Matt 28:18.] the Lord Christ, who has all fullness of grace in his heart and all fullness of power in his hand, is able to slay all these enemies of his. There is enough provision in him for my relief and help. He can take my drooping, dying soul and make me more than a conqueror.#footnote[Rom 8:37.] ‘Why do you say, O my soul, “My way is hidden from the #smallcaps[Lord], and my claim is ignored by my God”? Do you not know? Have you not heard? The #smallcaps[Lord] is the everlasting God, the Creator of the ends of the earth. He will not grow tired or weary; His understanding is beyond searching out. He gives power to the faint and increases the strength of the weak. Even youths grow tired and weary, and young men stumble and fall. But those who wait upon the #smallcaps[Lord] will renew their strength; they will mount up with wings like eagles; they will run and not grow weary, they will walk and not faint’ (Isa 40:27–31). He can make ‘the parched ground’ of my soul ‘become a pool,’ and my thirsty, barren heart ‘springs of water.’ Indeed, he can make this ‘haunt where jackals once lay,’ this heart so full of abominable lusts and fiery temptations, a place of ‘grass’ and fruit for himself” (Isa 35:7). So God held Paul up under his temptation with the thought of the sufficiency of his grace: “My grace is sufficient for you” (2 Cor 12:9). Though Paul was not at once given so much of it as to be freed from his temptation, yet its sufficiency in God for that end was enough to steady his spirit. I say, then: by faith, think much on the supply that is in Jesus Christ and its fullness, and how he can at any time give you strength and deliverance. Now if by this you do not find success to the point of victory, yet you will be kept upright in the chariot, so that you will not flee from the field until the battle is over. You will be kept from utter despondency, from lying down under your unbelief, or from turning aside to false means and remedies that in the end will not relieve you. The power of this consideration will be found only in practice.

(2.) By faith, lift up your heart to #emph[expect relief] from Christ. Relief from Christ in this case is like the prophet’s vision: “For the vision awaits an appointed time; it testifies of the end and does not lie. Though it lingers, wait for it, since it will surely come and will not delay” (Hab 2:3). Though it may seem long to you while you are in your trouble and perplexity, it will surely come at the time the Lord Jesus has appointed, which is the best time. So if you can lift your heart to a settled expectation of relief from Jesus Christ, if your eyes are on him “as the eyes of servants look to the hand of their master”#footnote[Ps 123:2.] when they expect to receive something from him, your soul will be satisfied. He will surely deliver you. He will slay the lust, and your end will be peace. Only look for it from his hand, and wait for when and how he will do it.#footnote[Isa 7:9.] “If you#super[+] do not stand firm in your#super[+] faith, then you#super[+] will not stand at all.”

But you will say, “What ground do I have to build such an expectation on, so that I may expect not to be deceived?”

You have necessity to drive you on this course: you must be relieved and saved this way or not at all. To#footnote[John 6:68.] whom will you go? And there are also countless things in the Lord Jesus to encourage you and draw you to this expectation.

As for its necessity, I have partly shown it already, when I made clear that this is the work of faith and of believers only. “Apart from Me you#super[+] can do nothing,” says Christ (John 15:5), speaking with special reference to the purging of the heart from sin (verse 2). The mortification of any sin must come by a supply of grace; of ourselves we cannot do it. Now “God was pleased to have all His fullness dwell in Him” (Col 1:19), so that “from His fullness we have all received grace upon grace” (John 1:16). He is the head from whom the new man must receive the influences of life and strength, or it will decay every day. If#footnote[Col 1:11.] we are strengthened with power in the inner being, it is by Christ dwelling in our hearts through faith (Eph 3:16, 17). I have also shown before that this work is not to be done without the Spirit. From where, then, do we expect the Spirit? From whom do we look for him? Who has promised him to us, having obtained him for us? Ought not all our expectations in this to be on Christ alone? So let this be fixed in your heart: if you do not have relief from him, you will never have any. All ways, efforts, and strivings that are not driven by this expectation of relief from Christ, and from him only, are to no purpose and will do you no good. Indeed, if they are anything other than supports of your heart in this expectation, or means he himself has appointed for receiving help from him, they are in vain.

Now, to draw you further to this expectation:

(1.) Consider his #emph[mercifulness], tenderness, and kindness, as he is our great High Priest at the right hand of God. Certainly he pities you in your distress. He says, “As a mother comforts her son, so will I comfort you#super[+]” (Isa 66:13). He has the tenderness of a mother toward a nursing child. Heb 2:17, 18: “For this reason He had to be made like His brothers in every way, so that He might become a merciful and faithful high priest in service to God, in order to make atonement for the sins of the people. Because He Himself suffered when He was tempted, He is able to help those who are being tempted.” How is Christ’s ability, on account of his suffering, set before us? “Because He Himself suffered when He was tempted, He is able.” Did the sufferings and temptations of Christ add to his ability and power? No doubt not, considered absolutely and in itself. But the ability mentioned here is one that has readiness, inclination, and willingness to put itself forth going along with it; it is an ability of will against all that would dissuade. Having suffered and been tempted, he is able to break through everything that would dissuade him, to relieve poor tempted souls: Δύναται βοηθῆσαι, “He is able to help.” It is a metonymy, naming the effect: he can now be moved to help, having been tempted himself. So in ch. 4:15, 16: “For we do not have a high priest who is unable to sympathize with our weaknesses, but we have one who was tempted in every way that we are, yet was without sin. Let us then approach the throne of grace with confidence, so that we may receive mercy and find grace to help us in our time of need.” The exhortation of verse 16 is the same one I am pressing: that we should hold on to expectations of relief from Christ. The apostle there calls it χάριν εἰς εὔκαιρον βοήθειαν, “grace for seasonable help.” “If ever help was seasonable,” says the soul, “it would be so for me in my present condition. This is what I long for: grace for seasonable help. I am ready to die, to perish, to be lost forever. Iniquity will prevail against me if help does not come.” The apostle says, “Expect this help, this relief, this grace from Christ.” But on what ground? The one he lays down in verse 15. And we may observe that the word in verse 16 that the older English translation renders “obtain” is λάβωμεν: Ἵνα λάβωμεν ἔλεον, “That we may receive it”; suitable and seasonable help will come. I will say freely: this one thing, establishing the soul by faith in the expectation of relief from Jesus Christ,#footnote[Matt 11:28.] on account of his mercifulness as our high priest, will do more to ruin your lust and disorder, and bring a better and speedier result, than all the severest means of punishing the body that any of the sons of men ever bound themselves to. Indeed, let me add that no soul ever did or ever will perish by the power of any lust, sin, or corruption who could lift his soul by faith to an expectation of relief from Jesus Christ.#footnote[Isa 55:1–3; Rev 3:18.]

(2.) Consider the #emph[faithfulness] of Him who has promised; this may lift you up and confirm you in waiting with an expectation of relief. He has promised to relieve in such cases, and he will fulfill his word to the utmost. God tells us that his covenant with us is like the “fixed order” of heaven, the sun, moon, and stars, which have their sure courses (Jer 31:36). So David said that he watched for relief from God “more than watchmen wait for the morning,”#footnote[Ps 130:6.] something that will certainly come in its appointed time. So will your relief from Christ. It will come in its season, like the dew and rain on parched ground; for he who has promised is faithful. Particular promises to this purpose are countless. Let the soul always be furnished with some of them that seem especially to suit its condition.

Now there are two great advantages that always go with this expectation of help from Jesus Christ:

\[1.\] It engages him to give full and speedy help. Nothing engages a man’s heart more to be useful and helpful to another than that other’s expectation of help from him, if it has been rightly raised and encouraged by the one who is to give the relief. Our Lord Jesus has raised our hearts to this expectation by his kindness, care, and promises. Surely our rising up to it must be a great engagement on him to help us accordingly. The Psalmist gives us this as a proven maxim: “Those who know Your name trust in You, for You, O #smallcaps[Lord], have not forsaken those who seek You.” When the heart is once won to rest in God, to lay itself on him, he will certainly satisfy it. He will never be like water that fails; nor has he ever said to the descendants of Jacob, “Seek Me in vain.” If Christ is chosen as the foundation of our supply, he will not fail us.

\[2.\] It engages the heart to attend diligently to all the ways and means by which Christ usually communicates himself to the soul; and so it takes in the real help of all graces and ordinances whatever. He who expects anything from a man applies himself to the ways and means by which it may be obtained. The beggar who expects alms lies at the door, or in the way, of the man he expects it from. The way and the means by which Christ communicates himself are ordinarily his ordinances; he who expects anything from him must wait on him in them. It is the expectation of faith that sets the heart to work. It is not an idle, groundless hope I am speaking of. Now if there is any vigor, effectiveness, and power in prayer or sacrament for this end of mortifying sin, a man will certainly have his share in it all through this expectation of relief from Christ. For this reason I bring all particular exercises, such as prayer, meditation, and the like, under this head, and so I will not dwell on them further, when they are grounded on this foundation and spring from this root. They are of singular use for this purpose, and not otherwise.

Now for this direction for mortifying a prevailing disorder, you#super[+] may have a thousand “probatum est”s [it is proved]. Who has walked with God under this temptation and not found its use and success? I dare leave the soul with it, without adding anything more. Only some particulars relating to it may be mentioned:

First, exercise faith #emph[especially on the death], blood, and cross of Christ; that is, on Christ as crucified and slain. The mortification of sin comes especially from the death of Christ. It is one particular, indeed outstanding, purpose of the death of Christ, and it will certainly be accomplished by it. He died to destroy the works of the devil. Whatever came on our natures through the devil’s first temptation, whatever gains strength in our persons by his daily suggestions, Christ died to destroy it all. “He gave Himself for us to redeem us from all lawlessness and to purify for Himself a people for His own possession, zealous for good deeds” (Titus 2:14). This was his aim and intention in giving himself for us, and in it he will not fail: his design was that we might be freed from the power of our sins and purified from all our defiling lusts. Christ “loved the church and gave Himself up for her to sanctify her, cleansing her …, and to present her to Himself as a glorious church, without stain or wrinkle or any such blemish, but holy and blameless” (Eph 5:25–27). And this will be accomplished, by virtue of his death, in various and different degrees. That is why our washing, purging, and cleansing are everywhere ascribed to his blood (1 John 1:7; Heb 1:3; Rev 1:5). Sprinkled on us, it will “purify our consciences from works of death, so that we may serve the living God” (Heb 9:14). This is what we aim at, what we are pursuing: that our consciences may be purged from works of death, that these may be rooted out and destroyed and have no more place in us. This will certainly be brought about by the death of Christ; power will go out from there for this purpose. Indeed, all supplies of the Spirit, all communications of grace and power, come from there, as I have shown elsewhere.#footnote[Communion with Christ, chapters 7 and 8.] The apostle sets it out like this. In Rom 6:2 the case we have in hand is put: “How can we who died to sin live in it any longer?” That is: “Dead to sin by profession; dead to sin by the obligation to be so; dead to sin by sharing in the virtue and power for killing it; dead to sin by union with Christ and interest in him, in whom and by whom it is killed: how shall we live in it?” He presses this in the verses that follow by several considerations, all taken from the death of Christ. This must not be: verse 3, “Or aren’t you#super[+] aware that all of us who were baptized into Christ Jesus were baptized into His death?” In baptism we have an evidence of our being planted into Christ; we are baptized into him. But into a share in what of his are we baptized? “His death,” he says. If indeed we are baptized into Christ, beyond outward profession, we are baptized into his death. The apostle explains what it is to be baptized into the death of Christ in verses 4 and 6: “We were therefore buried with Him through baptism into death, in order that, just as Christ was raised from the dead through the glory of the Father, we too may walk in newness of life. … We know that our old man was crucified with Him so that the body of sin might be rendered powerless, that we should no longer be slaves to sin.” “This,” he says, “is our being baptized into the death of Christ: our conformity to it. It is to be dead to sin, to have our corruptions mortified, as he was put to death for sin; so that as he was raised up to glory, we may be raised up to grace and newness of life.” In verse 6 he tells us where this baptism into the death of Christ comes from; it comes from the death of Christ itself: “Our old man was crucified with Him so that the body of sin might be rendered powerless.” συνεσταυρώθη, “was crucified with Him,” not in respect of time, but of causality. We are crucified with him #emph[meritoriously], in that he obtained the Spirit for us to mortify sin; #emph[effectually], in that power comes from his death for our crucifying; and by way of #emph[representation] and #emph[example], we shall certainly be crucified to sin, as he was for our sin. This is what the apostle intends. Christ by his death, destroying the works of the devil and obtaining the Spirit for us, has so killed sin as to its reign in believers that it will not achieve its end and dominion.

Secondly, then, exercise faith on the death of Christ under these two aspects: first, in expectation of #emph[power], and secondly, in striving for #emph[conformity].#footnote[Phil 3:10; Col 3:3; 1 Pet 1:18, 19.] For the first, the general direction already given may be enough. As for the second, the apostle’s words in Gal 3:1 may give us some light for our direction. Let faith look on Christ in the gospel as he is set forth dying and crucified for us. Look on him under the weight#footnote[1 Cor 15:3; 1 Pet 1:18, 19; 5:1, 2; Col 1:13, 14.] of our sins, praying, bleeding, dying. By faith bring him in that condition into your heart; apply his blood, so shed, to your corruptions. Do this daily. I could draw out this consideration at great length, in several particulars, but I must come to a close.

==== The Work of the Spirit

2\. I have only, then, to add the main points of the work of the Spirit in this business of mortification, which is so especially ascribed to him.

In a word: this whole work, which I have described as our duty, is brought about, carried on, and accomplished by the power of the Spirit, in all its parts and degrees. For example:

(1.) He alone #emph[clearly and fully convicts] the heart of the evil, guilt, and danger of the corruption, lust, or sin to be mortified. Without this conviction, or while it is so faint that the heart can wrestle with it or digest it, no thorough work will be done. An unbelieving heart (and in part we all have such a heart) will find a way around any consideration until it is overpowered by clear and evident convictions. Now this is the proper work of the Spirit: “He will … convict … in regard to sin” (John 16:8). He alone can do it. If men’s rational considerations, together with the preaching of the letter, could convict them of sin, we would perhaps see more convictions than we do. Through the preaching of the word, men come to recognize that they are sinners, that such and such things are sins, and that they themselves are guilty of them. But this light is not powerful. It does not take hold of the practical principles of the soul so as to conform the mind and will to them, and to produce effects suitable to such an awareness. That is why wise and knowing men who lack the Spirit do not think those things to be sins at all in which the chief movements and workings of lust consist. It is the Spirit alone who can do this work to the purpose, and who does it. And this is the first thing the Spirit does toward the mortification of any lust whatever. He convicts the soul of all its evil, cuts off all its pleas, exposes all its deceits, stops all its evasions, answers its excuses, and makes the soul own its abomination and lie down under the sense of it. Unless this is done, all that follows is in vain.

(2.) The Spirit alone reveals to us #emph[the fullness of Christ] for our relief. It is this consideration that keeps the heart from false ways and from despairing despondency (1 Cor 2:8).

(3.) The Spirit alone #emph[establishes] the heart in the expectation of relief from Christ, which, as has been shown, is the great sovereign means of mortification (2 Cor 1:21).

(4.) The Spirit alone brings the #emph[cross] of Christ into our hearts with its sin-killing power; for it is by the Spirit that we are baptized into the death of Christ.

(5.) The Spirit is the author and finisher of our #emph[sanctification]. He gives new supplies and influences of grace for holiness and sanctification when the contrary principle is weakened and brought low (Eph 3:16–18).

(6.) In all the soul’s approaches to God in this condition, it has #emph[support] from the Spirit. Where do the power, life, and vigor of prayer come from? Where does its power to prevail with God come from? Is it not from the Spirit? He is “a Spirit of grace and prayer,” promised to those who “look on Me, the One they have pierced” (Zech 12:10), enabling them to pray “with groans too deep for words” (Rom 8:26). This is acknowledged to be the great means, or way, by which faith prevails with God. This is how Paul dealt with his temptation, whatever it was: “I pleaded with the Lord to take it away from me.”#footnote[2 Cor 12:8.] What the work of the Spirit in prayer is, from where and how he gives us help and makes us prevail, and what we must do to enjoy his help for that purpose, it is not my present intention to show.
